(function(){'use strict';const library=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!library||!Array.isArray(library.series))return;const changes={"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-22":{"number":22,"title":"Soundcheck","content":"The microphone made Greg sound like an angry duck.\n\nHe leaned toward it, said, \"Check, one, two,\" and the speaker answered with a noise that sent a pair of gulls flying from the roof. I nearly fell off the edge of the low stage laughing.\n\n\"Love that for us,\" he said, looking up at the sound technician. \"A new genre.\"\n\nThe Beach City boardwalk was busy even before lunch. The fundraiser organizers had arranged folding chairs and a donation table. Someone was carrying a box of paper cups up the steps while another volunteer tried to stop the wind from taking the event schedule into the ocean.\n\nThe technician crouched to examine the cable. The plug was cracked.\n\n\"That's a replacement,\" she said. \"And until we've tested the new one, nobody plugs anything into this channel.\"\n\nGreg rubbed his thumb along the worn edge of his guitar case. \"That channel has been making my voice sound like a goose for years. I thought it was just artistic range.\"\n\nThe technician didn't laugh. She took the power lead out of his hand.\n\n\"Seriously,\" she said. \"You're not going to get a funny noise if it shorts into the metal stand.\"\n\nHe looked at the cable again. The smile left his face.\n\n\"Okay. Sorry. Didn't know it was that bad.\"\n\nThe organizer was already asking whether the delay meant one fewer song. Greg began to explain that he could play acoustic; then he stopped and looked at the spare amp's rating.\n\n\"I can do a short acoustic set while she tests the gear,\" he said. \"But don't advertise a full electric show until we know it can happen.\"\n\nThe organizer made a face. \"People came for the old hits.\"\n\n\"Some of them did.\" Greg shrugged, awkwardly enough to make me smile. \"They still get me. That's either a bargain or a warning.\"\n\nWe helped the technician lay a dry cable route behind the platform. Greg wanted to coil the broken lead into the spare box, and she made him put a bright tag on it so it couldn't be used by mistake.\n\nHe wrote FAILED on the tag in huge letters. Then he added a tiny frowning guitar.\n\n\"You've given it a personality,\" I said.\n\n\"Yeah, well, now nobody's going to invite it back.\"\n\nHe made a soft rhythm with the heel of his shoe while we waited. Not the grand patience of a tea master; the restless timing of a man who could only keep still when a song needed him to.\n\n\"That needs replacing, not twisting until it works,\" she said.\n\nGreg looked guilty. \"I might have been planning to twist it.\"\n\n\"I could tell.\"\n\nI helped move the spare chairs out of the walkway while she fetched a replacement. The event's organizer, a man in a pale shirt, came over to ask Greg whether he would stick to the old popular songs. They'd promised the donors familiar material.\n\nGreg rubbed the back of his neck. \"I was thinking of doing one new one.\"\n\n\"People are coming to hear Mr. Universe. You know, the fun stuff.\"\n\nI saw the way his smile remained in place even after the rest of his face went quiet.\n\n\"Could we talk about the running order after the cable's fixed?\" I asked.\n\nThe organizer checked his watch, sighed, and agreed.\n\nGreg stayed behind on the stage, looking at the set list. He had written my name beside the last song because we'd practiced a little harmony together. I didn't know whether I was more frightened of singing in public or of disappointing him.\n\n\"You don't have to do it,\" he said, catching me looking.\n\n\"I want to. I just don't want to make a fool of us.\"\n\n\"We're gonna be on stage. Making a fool of ourselves is sort of the deal.\"\n\nThe replacement cable arrived. The technician taped down the cord where it crossed the rear stage, then asked everyone to test the sound with the actual number of instruments we'd use.\n\nGreg played the first chord. This time it sounded like a guitar.\n\n\"Unbelievable,\" he said. \"Technology.\"\n\nI took my place beside him. The harmony was rough at first, because I came in a beat late. He stopped, but instead of apologizing for me, he asked where I wanted the cue.\n\n\"Look at me before the second line,\" I said.\n\nHe nodded, and we tried again. It worked. That tiny accommodation felt larger than the whole stage.\n\nThe organizer returned. He had heard the rehearsal. \"That new song doesn't sound like your old work.\"\n\n\"No,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Wouldn't a crowd-pleaser be safer?\"\n\nGreg glanced at me, then back at the man. \"I can play the songs people know. But I want one that means something to me now.\"\n\nThe organizer looked at the donation table and the sparse rows of chairs.\n\n\"One new song,\" he conceded. \"Please don't turn the rest into an experiment.\"\n\nGreg agreed. He wasn't happy about the compromise, but it gave the set somewhere to begin.\n\nThe organizer called just as we were throwing away the lemonade cups.\n\n\"One more thing,\" he said, looking anxious. \"The donor wants a photograph of the two of you before the show. Something romantic. It might bring in more money.\"\n\nGreg stared at him. I felt my shoulders rise.\n\n\"We can take a photograph of the event,\" I said. \"Of people who agree to be in it.\"\n\nThe organizer glanced toward the stage. \"They're giving us a lot.\"\n\n\"I know. But a donation doesn't buy our relationship.\"\n\nGreg took a breath. \"Look, I want the instruments repaired too. That's why I said yes to playing. I didn't say yes to putting Jasper on a poster.\"\n\nThe man looked embarrassed, not cruel. He scratched a line off the clipboard and asked if a picture of the newly repaired instruments would work instead. We suggested he ask the volunteers first.\n\nAfter he left, Greg leaned against the railing.\n\n\"I almost said yes because I didn't want to sound ungrateful,\" he admitted.\n\n\"That wouldn't make you a bad person. I'm glad you stopped.\"\n\nHe rubbed his hand over his face. \"You were clear about being ethically non-monogamous and preferring polyamory from the start. I don't want to turn our relationship into the kind of story where somebody wins you.\"\n\n\"Then don't.\"\n\nHe laughed softly. \"Simple as that?\"\n\n\"Not simple. Just the next thing to do.\"\n\nWe went back to the stage and helped the technician test the replacement cable. She had us stand at different positions while Greg spoke into the microphone, so everyone could hear whether the crackling had stopped. It had.\n\n\"Good,\" she said. \"Now the duck can retire.\"\n\nGreg looked offended. \"That was my experimental sound.\"\n\nI laughed, and when he reached for my hand I took it. The music night would still happen, and we could still be seen together without being offered as the prize for a successful fundraiser.\n\nWe had forty minutes before the doors opened. Greg asked me to walk along the boardwalk with him. We bought lemonade from a stall and sat where the sea breeze wasn't quite strong enough to steal the cups.\n\n\"That guy hit a nerve,\" I said.\n\nHe stared out at the water. \"Sometimes I feel like everyone's still waiting for the kid who thought a stage would fix his life. And I don't want to disappoint them.\"\n\n\"Does that include me?\"\n\nHe looked shocked. \"No! I mean, I hope not.\"\n\n\"You can disappoint me. You just have to talk to me about it.\"\n\n\"That's a terrible marketing pitch.\"\n\n\"Very niche audience.\"\n\nHe laughed, then touched the back of my hand. \"You really want to sing?\"\n\n\"I do. Not because I'm trying to prove I'm your only person.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nWe had talked about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory long before this day. I could love him and others without asking a crowd to vote on which relationship counted. But I also wanted to be here for his performance, and I wanted to make that choice without it turning into a grand declaration.\n\nThe fundraiser began. There were too few chairs at first and then somehow too many people. The technician kept the levels sensible, and the organizer stopped trying to change the set once the first song had started.\n\nGreg was nervous for half a verse. Then he relaxed into the music. He made one joke about the terrible microphone, and the technician called back that she was charging him extra for emotional distress.\n\nWhen the final song came, he looked at me before the second line.\n\nI came in on time.\n\nThe applause wasn't enormous. A little kid near the front danced anyway, with the wholehearted confidence of someone who had never been told to be embarrassed. The donation table collected enough to repair the community instruments they'd brought out for the event.\n\nBackstage, Greg hugged me and then stepped back.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nIt was brief and a little clumsy because I was still holding the second guitar. He laughed into my hair, and I wanted to stay there for a whole minute longer.\n\nThen the technician asked us to help coil the cables. We did. The organizer thanked the volunteers by name rather than calling them a single happy family. Greg copied the set list for his notebook and left my name where it was.\n\nOn the walk home, he took my hand.\n\n\"Same time next gig?\"\n\n\"Ask me when you've got a date.\"\n\n\"Right. Consent and calendars. I'm learning.\"\n\n\"So am I.\"","word_count":1599},"su-43":{"number":43,"title":"Steven Comes Home for Dinner","content":"Steven arrived with a casserole dish, a travel bag, and a question he had apparently been saving for three weeks.\n\n\"Does Dad really have a concert hall in another dimension now?\"\n\nGreg looked at the dish.\n\n\"Hey, buddy.\"\n\n\"Hi, Dad.\"\n\nThey hugged in the doorway of the car wash office, Greg's guitar case pressing awkwardly between them. Steven had grown into the kind of adult who could carry his own worries without announcing them, but the expression on his face was still bright when he saw his father.\n\nI stood beside the kitchenette, trying not to feel like a furniture arrangement that had been added since his last visit.\n\nSteven turned toward me.\n\n\"Jasper, right?\"\n\n\"That's me.\"\n\n\"Dad's told me about you.\"\n\n\"All good things, I hope.\"\n\n\"Mostly. There was a very long story about a chair.\"\n\nI covered my face.\n\nGreg looked proud.\n\nDinner was supposed to happen at the little house near the beach. We'd bought vegetables and bread that morning. Greg had promised to cook; I had agreed to supervise because he had once described a microwave timer as a flexible suggestion.\n\nSteven brought the casserole because he knew us both well enough to anticipate disaster.\n\nHe set it on the counter and looked around the room. Greg had left three mismatched plates out, one of them with a chipped edge.\n\n\"Wow,\" Steven said. \"You two really are going with the classic Dad approach. Food first, inventory later.\"\n\n\"I made a list,\" Greg protested.\n\n\"You made a song about making a list.\"\n\n\"That still counts as planning!\"\n\nSteven opened the cupboard, found the oven gloves and handed them to me. He wasn't trying to take over the house. He was simply the only one who had noticed the casserole was hot.\n\nLater, while Greg washed the dishes, Steven stepped onto the porch with me. He asked whether I had really been to Lanternweald, and I told him the doors were real but didn't belong to any of us.\n\n\"Do people expect you to fix things when you get there?\" he asked.\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\nHe considered that. \"I know that feeling. I'm trying not to volunteer for every problem anymore.\"\n\n\"How's that going?\"\n\n\"Depends on the day.\" He gave a crooked smile. \"I love my dad. I also had to leave Beach City to work out what I wanted when nobody needed me for a minute.\"\n\nI didn't tell him that was exactly the right lesson or try to make his life into an inspiring anecdote. He'd lived it.\n\n\"Does your dad know that?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it. We're working on the rest.\"\n\nBehind us Greg was singing badly about a sponge. Steven laughed, then grew quiet again.\n\n\"There's something I want you to know,\" he said. \"I'm happy he's got people in his life. But I want to get to know you as you, too. Not just as Dad's new person.\"\n\n\"I'd like that.\"\n\nWhen we went back inside, Greg asked what we'd been talking about.\n\n\"Top-secret casserole business,\" Steven told him.\n\nGreg gasped. \"A betrayal in my own kitchen.\"\n\nThey both laughed. I heard the family resemblance, but Steven was the one who had chosen not to make the conversation a performance.\n\nIt smelled of roasted squash and herbs. I set it on the counter and felt grateful for a practical offering that didn't require a speech.\n\nWe sat around the table as evening settled over Beach City. Through the open window came the sound of a car passing the boardwalk, followed by a gull with an opinion.\n\nSteven asked about the Lanternweald.\n\nGreg told him about Starwake Pier, the repaired railings, and the fundraiser. I told him about the seven neighborhoods and the fact that the magical doors did not turn the residents into fictional versions of themselves.\n\n\"So you can visit a place and still come back?\" Steven asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"The doors are invitations. Not obligations.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n\"Sounds nice.\"\n\nThen he looked at Greg.\n\n\"Does it ever get confusing?\"\n\nGreg rested his fork on his plate.\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\nSteven glanced toward me, then back to his father.\n\n\"People coming and going. Knowing where home is. You used to worry about that stuff a lot.\"\n\nFor once Greg did not fill the silence with a joke.\n\n\"Yeah,\" he said. \"I did. Still do, sometimes.\"\n\nSteven waited.\n\nI wanted to rescue the moment. I could have explained our relationship structure, described the agreements we'd made, and presented the whole thing as a reassuring story in which nobody ever felt jealous or uncertain.\n\nThat wouldn't have been fair to either of them.\n\nGreg took a breath.\n\n\"Jasper and I have talked about what we want. They're ethically non-monogamous. They prefer polyamory, and we've agreed to be honest about our own boundaries instead of making promises neither of us means.\"\n\nSteven looked at me.\n\n\"That works for you?\"\n\n\"It's the kind of relationship I choose,\" I said. \"But nobody else has to want it just because I do.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"And Dad?\"\n\nGreg smiled a little.\n\n\"I'm still learning. It's different from some things I've known. But Jasper's honest with me, and I get to say when I'm scared. That's a big deal.\"\n\nSteven looked down at his plate.\n\n\"I'm glad you have that.\"\n\nI saw Greg's shoulders loosen.\n\nThen Steven added, \"I'm not asking because I need to approve anything. I just want to know you're both okay.\"\n\n\"We're okay,\" Greg said.\n\nI glanced at him.\n\n\"Most days.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"Hey.\"\n\n\"Some days we disagree about chairs.\"\n\nSteven laughed.\n\nThe conversation shifted to the repaired pier. Steven wanted to see it. Greg offered to take him after dinner, but Steven looked exhausted from the journey.\n\n\"Maybe tomorrow,\" he said.\n\n\"Tomorrow's fine.\"\n\nI took the plates to the sink while Greg and Steven talked about music. Greg had written a new song that left room in the melody. Steven wanted to hear it when he wasn't eating.\n\nA little later I found Steven outside on the steps.\n\nThe night smelled of salt and cut grass. Lights shone along the boardwalk. He was sitting with his elbows on his knees, looking toward the water.\n\n\"Mind if I sit?\" I asked.\n\n\"Sure.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\nFor a while we watched a couple walk past with a dog that had a much better sense of where it wanted to go than its owners did.\n\n\"Do you think Dad feels like he's starting over?\" Steven asked.\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"He spent so long being the person who waited for everyone else.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I don't want him to be lonely.\"\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\nSteven rubbed his hands together.\n\n\"I've had to learn that I can't keep everyone from hurting. I know Dad's an adult. I know he's allowed to make his own choices. It's just weird to realize that the people who took care of you are still figuring things out too.\"\n\nI thought about Neri, Danse, Iroh, Severus, and everyone who had been old enough to seem certain but human enough to keep learning. I thought about my own first days at Tideglass, when I couldn't remember my name and still had to make decisions about what came next.\n\n\"Maybe that's what being alive looks like,\" I said.\n\nSteven smiled.\n\n\"That's a very Dad answer.\"\n\n\"Should I be concerned?\"\n\n\"Only if you start writing a song.\"\n\n\"I'd be a danger to the music industry.\"\n\nHe laughed. The sound was warm, easier than it had been inside.\n\nI didn't tell him what to think about the Lanternweald or his father's relationships. He hadn't asked me to. We sat until Greg appeared in the doorway holding a dish towel.\n\n\"There you are,\" he said.\n\n\"Plotting against you,\" Steven answered.\n\n\"I knew it.\"\n\nGreg stepped outside and leaned against the railing.\n\n\"You're both coming tomorrow?\" he asked.\n\nSteven nodded.\n\n\"Yeah. I want to see the pier.\"\n\nThe following morning, we went through the Starwake Door together. Greg showed Steven the little stage, the repaired railings, and the spot where the acoustic concert had ended under the lanterns. Steven ran a hand along the sturdy wood, inspected the storage hooks, and asked questions that told me he was paying attention to the practical details rather than expecting magic to solve every problem.\n\nAt the far end of the pier, Toph was arguing with Danse about the height of the new handrail.\n\n\"That looks right,\" Steven said.\n\n\"It took three meetings to look right,\" I told him.\n\n\"Only three?\"\n\n\"New record.\"\n\nGreg laughed.\n\nThen Steven looked back toward Beach City, visible through the doorway.\n\n\"I think I understand why you like having two places to go.\"\n\n\"More than two, sometimes,\" I said.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"And you still have to choose which one you're in.\"\n\n\"Every time.\"\n\nWe spent the morning walking through Lanternwell Square. Iroh offered tea. Mara gave Steven a piece of bread and refused to accept an elaborate thank-you. Maui attempted to impress him by lifting a heavy table and was firmly instructed to put it down because the table had been repaired yesterday.\n\nSteven grinned at that.\n\nBy afternoon he was ready to go home.\n\nGreg hugged him beside the door.\n\n\"Come back when you want,\" he said.\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"Or don't. You can call.\"\n\nSteven smiled.\n\n\"Love you, Dad.\"\n\n\"Love you too, kiddo.\"\n\nAfter he left, Greg stood looking at the closed door for a long moment.\n\n\"Good visit?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"I used to think being a good dad meant having all the answers.\"\n\n\"Did that work?\"\n\n\"Not even a little.\"\n\nI slipped my hand into his.\n\n\"Maybe it means showing up honestly.\"\n\nGreg's smile was quiet.\n\n\"Steven said something like that.\"\n\n\"He's smart.\"\n\n\"He gets it from me.\"\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nWe walked toward the pier, where the late sunlight caught the repaired wood. The doors behind us remained open to anyone who wished to return.\n\nIt was enough for today.","word_count":1660}},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-22":{"number":22,"title":"A Machine with a Name","content":"Nick Valentine smoked like someone had told him it was impossible for a synth to get lung cancer and he had taken that as a challenge.\n\nDanse disliked him immediately.\n\nNick noticed the stare before Danse spoke. He gave the Paladin's armor a long, professional examination, taking in the worn plating and the clean joints.\n\n\"Nice suit,\" he said. \"Must be comfortable knowing exactly what the world thinks you're supposed to be.\"\n\nDanse's jaw tightened. \"Do you have information about the missing equipment?\"\n\n\"Sure. I also have an office, a bill and a rule against taking orders from men who walk through my door and call me a malfunction.\"\n\nI knew Nick well enough to hear the edge under the joke. He had taken cases for people who were frightened of him and cases for people who hated synths until they needed something found. He didn't owe Danse a lesson in being human.\n\n\"Can we start with the invoice?\" I asked.\n\nNick tipped his hat to me. \"Now that's the kind of conversation a detective can work with.\"\n\nHe laid out the shipping dates and the serial numbers he had checked. Danse bent over the page and immediately began comparing them to his own records. For a few minutes they spoke in details: rail car, transfer point, missing crate, witness who had not agreed to be named in an official report.\n\nIt was almost a working relationship. Then Danse said he would need the witness's name for Brotherhood verification.\n\n\"No,\" Nick said.\n\n\"That's standard procedure.\"\n\n\"Not for my client.\"\n\nDanse looked at him as though the entire city had grown a second set of walls. Nick kept his hand on the invoice. The records were available. The person who had risked giving them was not.\n\nI watched two men who distrusted each other find one small piece of work they could do without surrendering the questions neither was ready to answer.\n\nNick returned the favor with professionalism.\n\nWe encountered him in Diamond City while following a lead on stolen Brotherhood equipment. He was leaning against a wall outside the detective agency, hat low, cigarette ember bright beneath the market lights.\n\n“Paladin,” Nick said.\n\n“Synth.”\n\n“Always nice to feel seen.”\n\nI closed my eyes briefly.\n\n“This is going to be a long evening.”\n\nNick looked at me.\n\n“You traveling with him voluntarily?”\n\n“Usually.”\n\nDanse crossed his arms.\n\n“We’re not here to socialize.”\n\n“No danger of that.”\n\nThe stolen equipment turned out to be in the hands of a group of raiders operating near Back Street Apparel. Nick had information. Danse needed it.\n\nThis created what I can only describe as tactical politeness.\n\nNick gave directions.\n\nDanse said thank you as if the words had been requisitioned from a limited supply.\n\nBefore we left, Nick said, “Try not to shoot anybody just for having the wrong factory parts.”\n\nDanse’s shoulders went rigid.\n\nI stepped between them.\n\n“Good talk.”\n\nOutside the city, Danse walked in silence for nearly a mile.\n\nThen he said, “You enjoy putting me in these situations.”\n\n“I did not invent Nick Valentine.”\n\n“You could have gotten the information elsewhere.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because he’s an Institute machine.”\n\n“He hates the Institute.”\n\n“That doesn’t change what he is.”\n\nThere it was again.\n\nI thought of Amos.\n\n“What is he?” I asked.\n\nDanse gave me an incredulous look.\n\n“A synth.”\n\n“That’s a noun. Try harder.”\n\n“A machine built to imitate a human.”\n\n“He has memories, preferences, friendships, a job, moral opinions, and apparently better manners than you.”\n\n“He was programmed.”\n\n“So were you.”\n\nDanse stopped walking.\n\nThe words had come out harsher than I intended.\n\nI held up a hand.\n\n“I mean trained. We’re all shaped by something.”\n\n“That is not the same.”\n\n“No. It isn’t.”\n\nHis face remained tight.\n\nI softened my voice.\n\n“But how much difference matters before you decide someone isn’t a person?”\n\nDanse stared at the road.\n\n“I don’t know why you keep pushing this.”\n\n“Because I care what you think.”\n\nThat landed harder than the argument.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“And because one day,” I continued, “I’m afraid your rules are going to ask you to hurt someone who doesn’t deserve it.”\n\n“You think I’m capable of that.”\n\nThe hurt in his voice stopped me.\n\n“That’s not what I said.”\n\n“It’s what you meant.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n“I think you’re capable of choosing differently if you let yourself look at the person in front of you before the category.”\n\nDanse’s jaw worked.\n\n“We have a mission.”\n\nConversation over.\n\nThe raider camp was violent enough to make philosophy irrelevant for an hour.\n\nWe recovered the equipment.\n\nOn the way back, a mechanical voice called for help from an alley.\n\nA damaged protectron lay beneath fallen masonry.\n\nDanse ignored it.\n\nI stopped.\n\n“What are you doing?”\n\n“It’s a robot.”\n\n“It asked for help.”\n\n“It’s running a programmed emergency routine.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\nI started lifting debris.\n\nDanse stared at me.\n\nThen swore and helped.\n\nWe freed the protectron.\n\nIt thanked us and wandered away announcing municipal safety regulations.\n\nDanse watched it go.\n\n“This proves nothing.”\n\n“I didn’t say it did.”\n\n“Then why are you smiling?”\n\n“Because you helped.”\n\n“Because you were going to injure yourself moving that slab alone.”\n\n“Whatever you need to tell yourself.”\n\nHe gave me the look.\n\nBack in Diamond City, Nick was still outside his office.\n\nWe returned his borrowed map.\n\nDanse hesitated.\n\nThen said, “Your information was accurate.”\n\nNick raised one eyebrow.\n\n“Careful. Keep talking like that and people will think we’re friends.”\n\n“We are not.”\n\n“Good. I have a reputation.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse almost did.\n\nAlmost.\n\nAs we walked away, he said, “I still don’t trust him.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“But I acknowledge he acted in good faith.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“That’s not nothing.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe slipped his hand briefly against the small of my back to guide me through the crowd.\n\nThe touch lasted only a second.\n\nA machine with a name, I thought.\n\nA man with rules.\n\nA future neither of us could see coming.\n\nNick found us again before we left Diamond City.\n\nNot because he wanted to continue the argument.\n\nBecause somebody had shot at him from the upper stands.\n\nThe bullet had clipped the side of his face, tearing synthetic skin away and exposing metal beneath. Nick appeared more irritated than hurt.\n\nDanse stopped dead.\n\nThe damage made every abstract thing between them visible.\n\nMachine.\n\nPerson.\n\nBoth, perhaps.\n\nNick touched the torn edge of his cheek.\n\n“Don’t suppose either of you know a good seamstress.”\n\nI winced.\n\nDanse stared.\n\n“What happened?” he asked.\n\nNick raised one eyebrow.\n\n“Concern, Paladin?”\n\n“Threat assessment.”\n\n“Of course.”\n\nA second shot struck the wall behind us.\n\nThreat assessment became immediate.\n\nDanse grabbed Nick by the coat and pulled him behind a concrete stall while I ducked the other way. The shooter fired again from a balcony.\n\nDanse could have let Nick take the round.\n\nHe did not.\n\nHe moved before belief had time to vote.\n\nWe chased the gunman through two alleys and lost him near the city gate. By the time we returned, Nick was sitting on an overturned bucket while Ellie fussed over the damage to his face.\n\nDanse hovered several feet away.\n\nNick looked at him.\n\n“Saved my hide.”\n\n“I prevented a hostile shooter from completing an attack inside a civilian settlement.”\n\n“Right. My mistake.”\n\nDanse crossed his arms.\n\nNick’s visible mechanical eye clicked as it focused.\n\n“Funny thing about reflexes,” he said. “Sometimes they know what you believe before you do.”\n\nDanse’s jaw tightened.\n\n“I would have done the same for anyone.”\n\n“That’s sort of my point.”\n\nThe silence that followed was almost impressive.\n\nEllie looked at me.\n\nI looked at the ceiling.\n\nThis was not my argument to win.\n\nNick eventually stood.\n\n“Thanks anyway, soldier.”\n\nDanse nodded once.\n\n“You’re welcome.”\n\nNot *synth*.\n\nNot *machine*.\n\nYou.\n\nWe left the agency a few minutes later.\n\nDanse made it halfway through the market before speaking.\n\n“I acted before identifying him.”\n\n“You identified a person being shot at.”\n\n“I identified an ally in the immediate situation.”\n\n“Okay.”\n\nHe frowned at my lack of argument.\n\n“That does not prove personhood.”\n\n“I didn’t say it did.”\n\n“You usually say something.”\n\n“I’m trying a radical new strategy where I let you think.”\n\n“That sounds manipulative.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\nWe stopped near the noodle stand. Steam rose into the evening air.\n\nDanse watched people move through the market.\n\n“What if he believes he’s a person because he was designed to?”\n\n“Then he believes it.”\n\n“That isn’t an answer.”\n\n“It’s the only one I have.”\n\nHe looked frustrated.\n\nI leaned against the counter.\n\n“What would convince you?”\n\n“That a synth is human?”\n\n“That a synth is a person.”\n\nDanse opened his mouth.\n\nClosed it.\n\nHe had standards for threats, orders, maintenance, marksmanship, rank. He had no field test for personhood.\n\n“I don’t know,” he admitted.\n\nThe words cost him.\n\nI nodded.\n\n“That’s somewhere to start.”\n\nHe looked at me sharply.\n\n“I am not changing my position.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You sound pleased.”\n\n“I am.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because ‘I don’t know’ is more honest than pretending the answer is easy.”\n\nDanse stared out at the market again.\n\nA child ran past chasing a ball. A ghoul trader argued over ammunition. Nick’s silhouette moved behind the agency window.\n\n“I still think synths represent a grave threat,” he said.\n\n“That’s not an irrational fear. The Institute literally replaces people.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“You agree?”\n\n“I agree the Institute is terrifying. I don’t agree that every person they build inherits the guilt.”\n\nDanse’s gaze returned to the window.\n\n“We may never agree on this.”\n\n“Maybe.”\n\nI nudged his shoulder.\n\n“You still saved Nick.”\n\n“He was standing in my line of responsibility.”\n\n“That phrase is going to do incredible work for you emotionally.”\n\nHis mouth twitched despite himself.\n\nNick had left us with one final remark.\n\n“Funny thing about names,” he said, adjusting his hat over the damage to his face. “They’re mostly promises people agree to keep calling you.”\n\nDanse had frowned at that.\n\nHe frowned at it for the next mile too.\n\n“What?” I finally asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“That’s become your least convincing word.”\n\nHe glanced at me. “Valentine talks too much.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“He also makes imprecise philosophical claims.”\n\n“Also yes.”\n\nA pause.\n\n“I understand what he meant.”\n\nI did not push.\n\nThe fact that he had kept thinking about it was enough.\n\nOn the road back to the airport, Danse stopped beside a ruined storefront.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\nHe looked at me with something almost vulnerable.\n\n“If I ever become the person you think I could be… don’t let me pretend I was always that person.”\n\nThe request startled me.\n\n“I won’t.”\n\n“I mean it.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThen we kept walking.\n\nNeither of us understood yet how cruelly literal that promise would become.\n","word_count":1800},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-43":{"number":43,"title":"A Refugee at the Yard","content":"The stranger at the workshop door wore an old Commonwealth coat with the insignia torn from the shoulder.\n\nThat was the first thing Danse noticed.\n\nThe second was the way they kept one hand near their satchel, as though someone had taught them to expect their belongings to be searched.\n\n\"Are you taking repair work?\" the stranger asked.\n\nDanse glanced at the bent metal housing they were carrying.\n\n\"Depending on the damage.\"\n\nThey set it carefully on the counter.\n\nIt was a pump regulator, patched twice, with a burn mark along one side.\n\nThe burn marked the housing where an improper bypass had overloaded the contacts. Danse turned the regulator over, pressed the seam and set it on the counter.\n\n\"Who attempted the last repair?\"\n\n\"I did,\" the stranger said.\n\n\"With what tools?\"\n\n\"A screwdriver and the wiring I had.\"\n\nDanse took a breath through his nose. He had once been the kind of man who could turn a question about tools into an interrogation about a person's entire life. I watched that reflex struggle with the one he had been building since his exile.\n\n\"All right,\" he said. \"That tells me what to inspect. It doesn't tell me you were wrong to try.\"\n\nThe stranger blinked.\n\nRenn came from the back room carrying a parts tray. He checked the regulator's stamped number and shook his head.\n\n\"We don't have the replacement today. I can order it, but I won't let anyone fit a scavenged contact without testing the load.\"\n\nDanse looked irritated. \"I can fabricate one.\"\n\n\"You can make a temporary housing,\" Renn corrected. \"You cannot make an approved contact rating out of your reputation.\"\n\nThe argument was sharp. It was also ordinary enough to make the stranger's shoulders drop a fraction.\n\nI pointed to the posted estimate and asked whether they wanted us to check the ordering cost before beginning the work. They said yes. No one asked where they had slept last night.\n\nDanse put the regulator down and began the work sheet with its number rather than the person's name.\n\n\"First, we find out what the equipment needs,\" he said.\n\nThe stranger touched the empty space where a uniform insignia had once been sewn. They didn't offer their history. We didn't ask them to pay for a repair with it.\n\n\"Can you fix it?\"\n\n\"I can assess it.\"\n\nThe stranger looked over Danse's shoulder at me.\n\n\"Does he run this place?\"\n\nDanse went very still.\n\nI understood the question before he did.\n\n\"We share the work,\" I answered. \"And we don't ask people to join a faction before we'll look at their equipment.\"\n\nThey exhaled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nTheir name was Ash. They had come through the Brasswatch Door with permission from a local courier, following a rumor that the workshop repaired things without demanding military loyalty. They wanted a regulator fixed, temporary shelter, and a job that did not require them to tell strangers what they were.\n\nDanse examined the housing.\n\n\"This part isn't the original.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Where did you get the replacement?\"\n\n\"A machine shop near Bunker Hill.\"\n\n\"That's a dangerous route.\"\n\nAsh's jaw tightened.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked at their face again and seemed to hear how his question had landed.\n\n\"Can I open the casing?\" he asked.\n\nAsh nodded.\n\nThat was a better question.\n\nWe took the regulator apart on a clean mat. The internal coil had been repaired with copper wire too thin for the load, and the contacts were worn almost through. Danse explained the problem without blaming the person who had made the last repair.\n\nAsh listened carefully.\n\n\"You can fix it?\"\n\n\"Yes. It will take a day.\"\n\n\"I can pay with work.\"\n\n\"What kind?\"\n\n\"Woodworking. Cleaning. Inventory. I learn quickly.\"\n\n\"Do you have references?\"\n\nAsh's expression changed.\n\nDanse saw it.\n\n\"That was a standard question,\" he said.\n\n\"In the Commonwealth, standard questions get people killed,\" Ash replied.\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\nOutside, the temporary water station creaked as someone filled a jug. Pip called a greeting to a neighbor. The world sounded entirely ordinary, which made the fear in Ash's voice harder to ignore.\n\n\"I didn't mean to interrogate you,\" Danse said.\n\nAsh looked at him.\n\n\"You sound like the Brotherhood.\"\n\nHe did not deny it.\n\n\"I was Brotherhood.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThe two words changed the room.\n\nDanse's hands stopped moving over the regulator. For a moment, I could see him reaching for the authority he once understood. Then he put both hands flat on the table.\n\n\"What do you need from us right now?\" he asked.\n\nAsh blinked.\n\n\"A place to stay tonight. A door that locks. No one asking me for a serial number.\"\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n\"We have a spare room behind the yard,\" I said. \"But Neri handles temporary housing. We can ask what is available, privately.\"\n\nAsh nodded.\n\n\"Will you tell anyone why I came?\"\n\n\"Only what you give us permission to tell,\" I said.\n\nThey looked at Danse.\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"I won't report you.\"\n\nAsh's face did not soften.\n\n\"That's a promise people make when they want something.\"\n\nDanse accepted the criticism without argument.\n\n\"Then judge me by what I do.\"\n\nBefore anyone began the afternoon work, Tamsin asked Ash to meet her at the public counter. She had heard the offer to pay in labor and wanted to make one point clear.\n\n\"We don't exchange shelter for work here,\" she said. \"The hall room is yours under its own rules. If you want a paid shift, we can talk about that separately.\"\n\nAsh stared at her. \"You're saying I don't owe you for the key?\"\n\n\"Not with your labor.\"\n\nThey looked at me, then at Danse, still waiting to find the condition nobody had mentioned.\n\nRenn brought out the posted workshop rates and a simple task list. There were two jobs Ash might qualify for: helping repair shelves under supervision and sorting incoming materials. The work had an hourly payment and could be declined. Ash chose the shelves, after asking whether the workshop would search their belongings before each shift.\n\n\"No,\" Danse said. \"We check tools for safety before work. That is different from searching a person's bag.\"\n\nAsh didn't smile, but some of the tension went out of their shoulders.\n\nTamsin gave them the contact for the civilian complaints desk, not a promise that Danse would personally resolve every disagreement. The practical distinction seemed to matter more than another speech about welcome.\n\n\"Can I start now?\" Ash asked.\n\n\"After you see the exit route and decide whether the job suits you,\" Renn replied.\n\nDanse stayed quiet through the orientation. Watching him allow someone else to set the conditions was almost stranger than hearing him speak gently. When Ash picked up the first shelf bracket, it was as a paid worker doing a job they'd chosen, not as a refugee proving themselves worthy of the room they'd already been offered.\n\nWe spent the afternoon working quietly.\n\nAsh proved good with wood. They fixed the crooked shelf that had irritated everyone for a week. Tamsin stopped by and approved the repair with a nod. Pip brought bread and water and did not ask questions.\n\nDanse kept his distance unless Ash needed an instruction. At one point, he caught himself saying \"You will\" and stopped.\n\n\"Would you be willing to check the left bracket?\" he asked instead.\n\nAsh's mouth twitched.\n\n\"Sure.\"\n\nLater, when Ash went to speak with Neri, Danse sat on the back steps with the old regulator in his hands.\n\n\"You knew,\" I said.\n\n\"Suspected.\"\n\n\"About Ash?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked at the torn insignia on the coat hanging by the workbench.\n\n\"I used to believe every synth was a threat. That there was no meaningful difference between identity and manufacture.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I know what it feels like to have someone decide what I am before I speak.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"That doesn't automatically make you safe for everyone who's been hurt by that belief.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe said it without flinching.\n\n\"I don't want Ash to forgive me,\" he continued. \"I want to make sure the repair is sound and that nobody uses this place to hunt them.\"\n\n\"That's a good place to start.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You don't have to reassure me.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\nHe gave a short, surprised laugh.\n\nThat evening, Neri arranged a private room for Ash near the square. The key belonged to Ash; nobody else would enter without asking. The housing register listed only the help Ash had consented to request.\n\nThe next day, we finished the regulator.\n\nDanse explained what he'd replaced and showed Ash how to inspect the contacts without dismantling the whole housing. Ash followed every step.\n\n\"Does it work?\" they asked.\n\n\"Under the tested load, yes. You'll need a new coil eventually.\"\n\n\"Then I'll come back if I need one.\"\n\n\"You can.\"\n\nThey looked up at him.\n\n\"Is that an invitation or an order?\"\n\nDanse paused.\n\n\"An invitation.\"\n\nAsh nodded.\n\nThey paid with two hours of shelf repairs and left through the Brasswatch Door after choosing a safe return time.\n\nNo one applauded. No one announced that a former Paladin had become a better man. A stranger had received a repair without surrendering their private history.\n\nThat was enough.\n\nWhen the workshop closed, I found Danse cleaning the tools.\n\n\"You've been quiet,\" I said.\n\n\"Thinking.\"\n\n\"Dangerous.\"\n\nHe set the wrench down.\n\n\"Do you ever wonder whether the people we've hurt would prefer we stayed away from them?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I think the best I can do is leave them alone.\"\n\n\"Sometimes that is the best thing.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"And the other times?\"\n\n\"Ask what they need. Believe the answer. Do the work.\"\n\nDanse was silent for a while.\n\nThen he reached for my hand.\n\n\"Will you stay for dinner?\"\n\n\"I'd like that.\"\n\nHe smiled, almost shy.\n\nWe went home without carrying the day's good deed like a medal. I liked him better for it.\n\nThe next morning, the repaired shelf was still straight.\n\nThere was no lesson written on it.\n\nIt simply held what people needed it to hold.","word_count":1685}},"after-always":{"after-always-15":{"number":15,"title":"The Half-Blood Prince","content":"I returned Snape's old Potions book with three paper markers tucked between the pages.\n\nOne marker was mine. The other two came from a conversation with Madam Pomfrey that Severus would have described as a conspiracy if he'd known about it beforehand.\n\nI hadn't asked her whether the Half-Blood Prince was a brilliant man. I had shown her a copy of an old margin note and asked what could happen if a student treated a clever experiment as a complete safety protocol.\n\n\"Depends on the experiment,\" she'd said. \"But students don't inherit the right to harm somebody because a textbook contains a better method.\"\n\nShe'd tapped the page where the measurement stopped short of documenting a reaction. \"This tells me what a clever pupil wanted to attempt. It doesn't tell me what happened to the person standing next to the cauldron.\"\n\nSo I brought the questions back.\n\nSeverus noticed the extra slips before I'd even sat down.\n\n\"How many members of the faculty have you recruited to harass me?\" he asked.\n\n\"Only one. She charges reasonable rates.\"\n\n\"Pomfrey.\"\n\n\"She has opinions about how you recorded risks.\"\n\n\"She has opinions about how everybody records risks.\"\n\nI could hear a memory of the Hogwarts infirmary inside the way he said her name: irritation, respect, and the long experience of someone who had been told to stop pretending precision made him exempt from consequences.\n\nHe opened to the first marked page.\n\n\"She is correct about the missing observation,\" he said, so quietly that I thought I'd misheard.\n\n\"Would you write that in the margin?\"\n\n\"No. I will attach a separate note. The original is evidence of what I thought then.\"\n\nHe took out a clean piece of paper. I watched his hand pause before he wrote. It was not an apology, but it was an admission made where another person could later read it without needing to believe in his goodness first.\n\nHe removed each one as if they were insects.\n\n“What are these?”\n\n“Questions.”\n\n“I did not invite annotations.”\n\n“They aren't annotations. They're removable.”\n\n“You have introduced foreign material into a thirty-year-old book.”\n\n“They're paper.”\n\n“So are bookworms' preferred meals.”\n\n“Are you comparing my questions to parasites?”\n\n“I am comparing your organizational habits to infestation.”\n\nI sat across from him.\n\n“First marker. Why 'Half-Blood Prince'?”\n\nHis expression closed.\n\nI had expected that.\n\n“You may refuse.”\n\nHe looked at me sharply.\n\n“Then why ask?”\n\n“Because you may not.”\n\nHe leaned back.\n\nThe book rested between us.\n\n“Prince was my mother's name.”\n\n“I know that much.”\n\n“Of course you do. Newspapers have excavated my genealogy with all the delicacy of grave robbers.”\n\n“They say your father was a Muggle.”\n\n“He was.”\n\n“And you were proud of your mother's name?”\n\nSnape's fingers touched the cover.\n\n“Pride is a childish word for it.”\n\n“You were a child.”\n\nHis gaze lifted.\n\nThat stopped him.\n\n“At first,” he said.\n\nI waited.\n\nHe looked past me toward the classroom shelves.\n\n“Prince sounded like something chosen instead of inherited. Something that belonged to magic rather than that house.”\n\nSpinner's End.\n\nThe narrow bedroom.\n\nThe gray street.\n\n“I could be clever there,” he continued. “In the margins. No one knew who had written them. No one cared what I wore, or where I lived, or whose son I was.”\n\n“Only whether the method worked.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI understood that more than I wanted to admit.\n\n“So you made yourself a title.”\n\n“A pretentious one.”\n\n“You were a teenager.”\n\n“That is not a defense.”\n\n“It is for pretension.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“Second marker,” I said.\n\nHe sighed with theatrical suffering.\n\n“You corrected a strengthening solution by reducing the powdered griffin claw. Why?”\n\n“Because too much causes brittleness in enchanted tissue.”\n\n“That's not in any reference I own.”\n\n“Then your references are incomplete.”\n\n“How did you discover it?”\n\n“Experiment.”\n\n“At sixteen?”\n\n“Seventeen.”\n\n“Much more sensible.”\n\nHis eyebrow rose.\n\nI turned the book toward him.\n\n“You were doing original research before leaving school.”\n\n“I was bored.”\n\n“That is not normal boredom.”\n\n“I have never been accused of normality.”\n\n“True.”\n\nThe third marker was the dangerous one.\n\nI hesitated before opening to it.\n\nSectumsempra.\n\nThe word sat in the margin like a scar.\n\nSnape's face hardened.\n\n“You said later,” I reminded him.\n\n“I did.”\n\n“This is later.”\n\n“So it is.”\n\nI did not ask why he invented it first.\n\nI asked, “How old were you?”\n\n“Sixteen.”\n\nMy stomach tightened.\n\n“What does it feel like to know something that violent came from you?”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\n“That is not the question people ask.”\n\n“What do they ask?”\n\n“Whether I regret it.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nNo hesitation.\n\nThe answer made the next question harder.\n\n“Would you erase it if you could?”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“No.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because erasing the spell from my past would not erase the person who needed to invent it.”\n\nI went still.\n\nSnape's voice was quiet.\n\n“I was angry. Humiliated. Fascinated by Dark magic. Surrounded by people who rewarded cruelty when it was clever enough. And I wanted a weapon that belonged to me.”\n\n“Against the Marauders?”\n\n“Against enemies.”\n\nThe old note.\n\nFor enemies.\n\n“That included them?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But not only them.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI traced the edge of the page with my gaze.\n\n“Harry used it on Draco.”\n\nSnape's jaw tightened.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You saved Draco.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What was that like?”\n\nHis eyes flashed.\n\n“Do you collect impossible questions?”\n\n“Professionally.”\n\nHe looked at the book.\n\n“To watch a curse I had made cut a boy who had no idea what it was,” he said slowly, “was clarifying.”\n\n“Did you blame Harry?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Did you blame yourself?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Did you tell him what the spell did afterward?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I was furious. Because he had stolen from me. Because I was protecting the identity of the Prince. Because I was not, contrary to your developing mythology, always wise.”\n\n“I have no such mythology.”\n\n“You have begun to look at me as though every unpleasant act conceals an admirable motive.”\n\nThe accusation stung because it was partly true.\n\nI sat back.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nHis brow furrowed.\n\n“For what?”\n\n“For catching me.”\n\nHe looked genuinely confused.\n\n“I don't want to make you easier than you are,” I said. “Not by turning everything into evidence for your guilt, and not by turning everything into evidence for your goodness.”\n\nHis gaze sharpened.\n\n“You believe there is a difference.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Between what?”\n\n“Understanding you and excusing you.”\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\nSnape's fingers remained on the book.\n\n“Most people prefer one of two stories,” he said at last. “Monster or martyr.”\n\n“Both are lazy.”\n\n“A sentiment you will find unpopular.”\n\n“I have survived worse.”\n\n“So I gather.”\n\nI closed the book.\n\n“Do you still think of yourself as the Half-Blood Prince?”\n\nHis expression shifted.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Never?”\n\n“Rarely.”\n\n“What does it mean now?”\n\nHe considered.\n\n“A boy who thought invention could make him untouchable.”\n\n“That is sad.”\n\n“It is accurate.”\n\n“Both can be true.”\n\nHis eyes met mine.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThat phrase again.\n\nBoth can be true.\n\nIt had become something between us.\n\nNot forgiveness.\n\nNot absolution.\n\nA refusal to flatten.\n\nSnape slid the book toward himself, then stopped.\n\n“You may keep it another week.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“You're serious?”\n\n“I am beginning to doubt the wisdom of it.”\n\n“I'll take excellent care of it.”\n\n“You will use no additional markers.”\n\n“I make no promises.”\n\n“Then give it back.”\n\nI pulled it against my chest.\n\n“Too late.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\nI smiled.\n\nFor a moment, he looked less like a former spy, former Headmaster, former Death Eater, former boy who had invented a curse for enemies.\n\nHe looked like a man watching someone steal his book and deciding, against every instinct, to allow it.\n\n“Insufferable,” he said.\n\n“Pretentious.”\n\n“Thief.”\n\n“Prince.”\n\nThat stopped him.\n\nThe word hung there, stripped of legend.\n\nNot title.\n\nNot accusation.\n\nSomething gentler.\n\nHis face changed.\n\nI felt heat rise into mine and looked down too late.\n\nSnape noticed.\n\nOf course he did.\n\nHe said nothing.\n\nLater, alone in my room, I opened the book again and studied the handwriting instead of the spells.\n\nSome notes were impatient corrections. Some were elegant improvements. Some were jokes so dry I could hear his adult voice in them. Others made my skin crawl.\n\nThat combination mattered.\n\nIt would have been easier if brilliance and cruelty occupied separate pages. Easier if there were a chapter where the lonely child ended and the dangerous young man began. The margins refused that comfort.\n\nThe next morning I returned the book to him open to a modified potion instruction.\n\n“This one,” I said. “Why twelve clockwise stirs instead of the printed seven?”\n\nHis suspicion gave way to interest almost instantly.\n\n“The author assumes a uniform cauldron wall temperature.”\n\n“And yours doesn't?”\n\n“No real cauldron does.”\n\nFor ten minutes he forgot to be guarded. He drew a diagram. He explained convection. He became animated enough to steal my quill and correct my notation.\n\nThen he seemed to realize what had happened and stopped.\n\nI looked at the diagram.\n\n“You like this.”\n\n“Competence?”\n\n“Potions.”\n\nHis expression closed halfway.\n\n“Yes,” he said.\n\nNot loved. Not once. Just yes.\n\nIt was enough to make the room feel brighter.\n\nThat silence had a new language too.","word_count":1538},"after-always-42":{"number":42,"title":"Not an Appointment","content":"Iroh invited Severus Snape to tea by sending him a formal card.\n\nIt was cream-colored, folded once, and bore a small green dragon stamped in the corner. The handwriting inside was beautiful.\n\nDear Professor Snape, it said. Your company would be welcome at the Jasmine Steps tasting on Thursday. No speeches are required.\n\nSeverus read the card twice.\n\nThen he put it facedown on the kitchen table.\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\nI was cutting an apple into pieces small enough to eat while sorting a stack of invoices. I did not look up.\n\n\"Are you declining the tea or the lack of speeches?\"\n\n\"The implication.\"\n\n\"Which implication?\"\n\n\"That I have become a public exhibit.\"\n\nI set down the knife.\n\n\"He's inviting you to drink tea.\"\n\n\"He is inviting the notorious surviving spy of the war into a room full of people who think an interesting guest constitutes an evening's entertainment.\"\n\nI folded the card and looked at the back. Nothing there suggested a lecture, a public question period, or a dramatic unveiling of Severus's biography.\n\n\"You could just say no,\" I said.\n\n\"I did.\"\n\n\"Then I'll tell him.\"\n\nSeverus turned toward me. He seemed almost disappointed by how easily I'd accepted his refusal.\n\n\"You intend to attend?\"\n\n\"Yes. I like tea.\"\n\n\"An insufficient reason to subject yourself to crowds.\"\n\n\"I also like Iroh.\"\n\nA small movement at the corner of his jaw told me he'd heard the choice of words. I gave him time to decide whether he wanted to ask the question behind it.\n\nHe did not.\n\nI went back to my apple.\n\nThat was not the end of the argument.\n\nIt followed us through breakfast, into the annex, and all the way to Moonroot Archive. Severus insisted he was not offended. He was merely unimpressed by the presumption of strangers who thought they were entitled to his time.\n\nI agreed that strangers were not entitled to his time.\n\nHe seemed to find this aggravating.\n\nNear noon, Iroh arrived in the archive carrying a basket of clean cups for the pantry.\n\nThe cups were carefully wrapped in a cloth marked with Mina's neat inventory numbers. Iroh had brought exactly the quantity on the loan sheet, neither more nor fewer. There was something almost comical about the care with which a former general carried a small stack of crockery.\n\nSeverus looked at the basket. \"Are we under siege by refreshments?\"\n\n\"Not today,\" Iroh replied cheerfully. \"There are several people in the district who would object to their tea being requisitioned.\"\n\n\"Then I trust they know where to find it.\"\n\n\"They do. I was delivering the cups to the pantry.\"\n\nFor once, Iroh didn't ask what Severus was working on. He placed the basket on the table beside Neri's receipt, moved one cup so it wouldn't wobble, and kept his hands to himself.\n\n\"I believe you have declined the tasting,\" he said.\n\n\"Quite thoroughly.\"\n\n\"Then I will not ask again. You are welcome on a different day should you change your mind.\"\n\nSeverus's eyes narrowed. He had been prepared for a philosophical argument, perhaps a lecture about tea and friendship. Iroh gave him an ordinary nod instead and turned back toward his work.\n\nWhen the older man was out of earshot, Severus muttered, \"Insufferably courteous.\"\n\n\"That's not the same as being wrong.\"\n\n\"I did not say it was.\"\n\nI had expected a duel of wise quotations. What happened instead was far more like both men: Iroh used the tact he might have employed in a delicate negotiation, and Severus resented the fact that the negotiation had been concluded without anyone trying to corner him.\n\nNeri signed the loan sheet. The cups went to their owners. No invitation became a summons. He greeted Neri, complimented Pip on a repaired door latch, and came toward our worktable with a smile.\n\n\"I hope the card reached you,\" he said.\n\n\"It did,\" Severus answered.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"I shall not be attending.\"\n\nIroh bowed his head slightly.\n\n\"Then I hope we may meet another day.\"\n\nSeverus looked as though someone had misplaced the rest of his argument.\n\n\"That is all?\"\n\n\"Would you prefer a lengthy attempt to change your mind?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then I shall spare us both.\"\n\nIroh turned to me.\n\n\"I will save a cup for anyone who arrives before the kettle cools. If you have time, Jasper, you are welcome.\"\n\n\"I'll be there.\"\n\n\"Wonderful.\"\n\nHe left the basket behind and went to help Mara set up the afternoon pantry shelves.\n\nSeverus stared after him.\n\n\"He is either genuinely polite or very skilled at appearing so.\"\n\n\"Both could be true.\"\n\n\"I dislike uncertainty.\"\n\n\"I've noticed.\"\n\nThe rest of the day was ordinary in ways that became important only later. We cataloged three damaged journals. Severus corrected a mislabeled vial and did not insult the apprentice who had made the error. Neri brought a photograph of the underside of the well's old stone arch, and we compared it with the drawing from the washed-up ledger fragment.\n\nOne line on the old map was blank.\n\nSeverus marked it, but did not guess what it meant.\n\nAt four o'clock, he closed his notebook.\n\n\"You should leave if you intend to be on time.\"\n\n\"Are you dismissing me?\"\n\n\"I am observing the clock.\"\n\n\"That is how you dismissed me yesterday.\"\n\n\"Then you have learned to recognize a pattern.\"\n\nI collected my bag and paused by the door.\n\n\"You could still come,\" I said. \"Not because anyone expects you to be charming. Just because you might like the tea.\"\n\nHe looked at me for a long moment.\n\n\"I would not like the people.\"\n\n\"Then enjoy your evening without them.\"\n\nHis expression softened slightly.\n\n\"Do not let them make you stay beyond your wishes.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\nI went alone.\n\nJasmine Steps was crowded with the gentle noise of people who were not trying to impress one another. Greg had brought a guitar but was sitting with it unplayed across his knees. Katara was helping a neighbor carry a tray; Toph had occupied the chair with the best view of the garden despite not being interested in views.\n\nIroh poured a cup for me.\n\n\"Severus sends his regrets,\" I said.\n\n\"Does he?\"\n\n\"More accurately, he sends an exceptionally firm no.\"\n\n\"Then I am glad he was able to give it.\"\n\nWe drank the tea. It tasted of mountain mint and something warm beneath it that I could not name.\n\nAfter a while, Iroh asked how the archive was doing.\n\nI told him about the mislabeled recipe, the corrected copy, and Neri's old drainage diagrams. I did not tell him which pages Severus had kept private. It felt good to speak about our work without opening the parts of it that did not belong to me.\n\nGreg played a short melody after the cups were cleared. Toph complained that one note was half a beat late.\n\n\"That was on purpose,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Then you made a mistake intentionally. Congratulations.\"\n\nAang laughed so hard he nearly dropped his cup.\n\nI stayed until the lanterns came on, then walked back toward Moonroot alone. I could have stayed longer. I could have gone to another person's door. My relationships did not turn every independent evening into evidence for or against anybody. I wanted to go home.\n\nSeverus was in the kitchen at Spinner's End when I arrived.\n\nThe kettle had already been filled.\n\n\"How was the exhibition?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very disappointing. No one asked me to juggle, and nobody tried to turn me into a display.\"\n\n\"A shame.\"\n\n\"Greg played music.\"\n\n\"Predictable.\"\n\n\"Toph criticized the rhythm.\"\n\n\"An entirely reasonable response.\"\n\nI hung up my coat.\n\n\"Did you have a good evening?\"\n\n\"Quiet.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"That is all you intend to ask?\"\n\n\"Did you want me to make an argument for attending next time?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then I won't.\"\n\nSeverus set two mugs on the table. He hesitated before speaking.\n\n\"I considered that you might interpret my refusal as disinterest in the people you care about.\"\n\n\"I didn't.\"\n\n\"Or as a demand that you decline invitations on my behalf.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't.\"\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"I prefer to know where I stand.\"\n\n\"You stand wherever you choose, Severus. You don't need to attend every gathering. And I don't need to pretend everyone I love has identical boundaries.\"\n\nSomething in his shoulders eased.\n\n\"I could perhaps tolerate a smaller visit,\" he said. \"At another time.\"\n\n\"Iroh would probably be delighted.\"\n\n\"Do not tell him I said delighted.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe moved toward me, paused, and lifted a hand to my cheek. His touch was careful, but there was nothing uncertain about the way he looked at me.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was brief, warm, and a little sharper when I smiled against his mouth. He rested his forehead against mine for one quiet beat.\n\nThe kettle began to whistle.\n\n\"That thing has the most appalling timing,\" I said.\n\n\"It has impeccable timing. You are simply distractible.\"\n\nThe tea was not as good as Iroh's.\n\nI told Severus so.\n\nHe informed me that my palate remained beyond repair.\n\nWe drank it anyway, together at a table where neither of us had been required to prove love by being anywhere we didn't wish to be.","word_count":1537}}};for(const story of library.series){const entries=changes[story.series_slug]||{};for(const chapter of story.chapters){const e=entries[chapter.id];if(!e||e.number!==chapter.chapter_number)continue;chapter.content=e.content;chapter.title=e.title;chapter.word_count=e.word_count;}}})();
